1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,662
Squeeze into a crowded bus, and
everyone shuffles to make room.

2
00:00:04,662 --> 00:00:08,631
Open a fizzy drink, and gas
rushes out to fill the space.

3
00:00:09,031 --> 00:00:11,942
Push on something balanced,
and it pushes back.

4
00:00:11,942 --> 00:00:13,772
An equilibrium does the same.

5
00:00:14,222 --> 00:00:16,511
Diya opens a pressure
cooker in her kitchen.

6
00:00:16,511 --> 00:00:18,518
The hiss stops, and steam pours out.

7
00:00:18,868 --> 00:00:22,181
Less pressure on top, so
more water turns into steam.

8
00:00:22,681 --> 00:00:26,798
By the end, you will predict what
any push does to an equilibrium, and

9
00:00:26,798 --> 00:00:28,179
how factories use it.

10
00:00:28,829 --> 00:00:31,101
In eighteen eighty four, Henri
Le Chatelier gave the rule.

11
00:00:31,451 --> 00:00:35,793
Change the conditions of a system at
equilibrium, and it shifts to undo

12
00:00:35,793 --> 00:00:36,693
the change.

13
00:00:37,085 --> 00:00:40,494
The pushes are concentration,
pressure and temperature.

14
00:00:40,494 --> 00:00:43,545
We will also test an
inert gas and a catalyst.

15
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:45,226
Think of a see-saw.

16
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:48,887
Add weight on one side, and
the balance moves to fight it.

17
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:54,399
Let's take hydrogen and iodine making
hydrogen iodide, at equilibrium on

18
00:00:54,399 --> 00:00:55,954
our molecule bench.

19
00:00:56,304 --> 00:00:58,004
Now we add more hydrogen.

20
00:00:58,404 --> 00:00:59,922
The system fights back.

21
00:00:59,922 --> 00:01:03,384
Hydrogen reacts with iodine
and makes more hydrogen iodide.

22
00:01:03,784 --> 00:01:07,360
The forward reaction runs
until Q comes back to K.

23
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:12,014
Take hydrogen iodide away instead,
and the same thing happens.

24
00:01:12,014 --> 00:01:13,463
More product forms.

25
00:01:13,863 --> 00:01:17,272
Add a substance, and the
equilibrium moves away from it.

26
00:01:17,272 --> 00:01:19,563
Remove one, and it moves toward it.

27
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:21,259
Next, pressure.

28
00:01:21,259 --> 00:01:22,731
Picture a crowded room.

29
00:01:22,731 --> 00:01:25,082
To save space, people stand in pairs.

30
00:01:25,432 --> 00:01:29,397
Squeeze a gas mixture, and the
equilibrium moves to the side with fewer

31
00:01:29,397 --> 00:01:30,344
gas molecules.

32
00:01:30,744 --> 00:01:34,727
Lower the pressure, and it moves to
the side with more gas molecules.

33
00:01:34,727 --> 00:01:36,336
Same number on both sides?

34
00:01:36,336 --> 00:01:37,502
No change at all.

35
00:01:38,152 --> 00:01:39,052
Why?

36
00:01:38,634 --> 00:01:41,263
Let's see it with numbers, step by step.

37
00:01:41,613 --> 00:01:42,513
Step one.

38
00:01:42,401 --> 00:01:46,341
Carbon monoxide and three hydrogen
make methane and water vapour.

39
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:49,608
Four gas molecules on the
left, two on the right.

40
00:01:50,358 --> 00:01:51,258
Step two.

41
00:01:51,209 --> 00:01:52,538
Halve the volume.

42
00:01:52,538 --> 00:01:54,437
Every concentration doubles.

43
00:01:55,187 --> 00:01:56,088
Step three.

44
00:01:56,088 --> 00:01:58,506
The top of Q has two concentrations.

45
00:01:58,506 --> 00:01:59,964
The bottom has four.

46
00:01:59,964 --> 00:02:02,089
So Q becomes one quarter of K.

47
00:02:02,839 --> 00:02:03,739
Step four.

48
00:02:03,632 --> 00:02:08,411
Q is now smaller than K, so the reaction
moves forward, toward fewer molecules.

49
00:02:09,161 --> 00:02:10,661
Now three more pushes.

50
00:02:11,011 --> 00:02:14,571
Add an inert gas, like
argon, at constant volume.

51
00:02:14,571 --> 00:02:17,065
The partial pressures do not change.

52
00:02:17,065 --> 00:02:18,224
Nothing shifts.

53
00:02:18,624 --> 00:02:20,099
Temperature is special.

54
00:02:20,099 --> 00:02:22,264
It changes the value of K itself.

55
00:02:22,664 --> 00:02:26,572
For a reaction that gives out
heat, heating moves it backward.

56
00:02:26,572 --> 00:02:28,436
Think of heat as a product.

57
00:02:28,836 --> 00:02:31,303
A catalyst speeds up
both directions equally.

58
00:02:31,303 --> 00:02:33,990
Equilibrium comes sooner,
but it does not move.

59
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:35,490
Compare.

60
00:02:35,282 --> 00:02:39,571
Brown nitrogen dioxide joins into
colourless dinitrogen tetroxide and gives

61
00:02:39,571 --> 00:02:40,471
out heat.

62
00:02:40,371 --> 00:02:42,379
In ice, the gas turns pale.

63
00:02:42,379 --> 00:02:44,231
In hot water, dark brown.

64
00:02:44,631 --> 00:02:45,740
Pause and think.

65
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:47,589
Making ammonia gives out heat.

66
00:02:47,589 --> 00:02:51,593
If we heat the mixture, does the
amount of ammonia go up or down?

67
00:02:54,793 --> 00:02:55,693
Down.

68
00:02:55,327 --> 00:02:57,044
Heat acts like a product.

69
00:02:57,044 --> 00:03:01,153
Adding it pushes the reaction
backward, so some ammonia breaks up.

70
00:03:01,803 --> 00:03:04,179
Now a real factory, the Haber process.

71
00:03:04,529 --> 00:03:07,251
Nitrogen and three
hydrogen make two ammonia.

72
00:03:07,251 --> 00:03:11,231
Delta H is minus ninety two point
three eight kilojoules per mole.

73
00:03:11,631 --> 00:03:13,222
Four molecules become two.

74
00:03:13,222 --> 00:03:16,361
So high pressure helps, about
two hundred atmospheres.

75
00:03:16,761 --> 00:03:19,833
It gives out heat, so
cold gives more ammonia.

76
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:24,533
But cold is too slow, so factories pick
about five hundred degrees Celsius.

77
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:26,903
An iron catalyst makes it fast.

78
00:03:26,903 --> 00:03:30,277
It does not change how much
ammonia forms at equilibrium.

79
00:03:30,277 --> 00:03:31,965
It just gets there sooner.

80
00:03:32,565 --> 00:03:33,564
Remember this.

81
00:03:33,564 --> 00:03:36,153
Push an equilibrium, and it pushes back.

82
00:03:36,853 --> 00:03:38,543
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:38,843 --> 00:03:43,198
K c is fifty seven at seven
hundred kelvin for hydrogen iodide.

84
00:03:43,198 --> 00:03:47,754
At equilibrium, hydrogen and iodine
are zero point one molar each.

85
00:03:47,754 --> 00:03:51,137
Then we add hydrogen to
make it zero point two.

86
00:03:51,637 --> 00:03:56,413
First, hydrogen iodide squared equals
fifty seven times zero point zero

87
00:03:56,413 --> 00:03:57,313
one.

88
00:03:56,972 --> 00:03:59,283
That is zero point five seven.

89
00:03:59,283 --> 00:04:03,622
So hydrogen iodide is about zero
point seven five five molar.

90
00:04:04,372 --> 00:04:06,684
Now hydrogen is zero point two.

91
00:04:06,684 --> 00:04:11,864
Q equals zero point five seven, over
zero point two times zero point one.

92
00:04:12,614 --> 00:04:14,702
That is twenty eight point five.

93
00:04:14,702 --> 00:04:15,874
Only half of K.

94
00:04:16,624 --> 00:04:21,256
Q is smaller than K, so the reaction
moves forward and uses up some extra

95
00:04:21,256 --> 00:04:22,156
hydrogen.

96
00:04:22,005 --> 00:04:23,828
Just as Le Chatelier said.

97
00:04:24,478 --> 00:04:25,939
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:26,289 --> 00:04:30,349
Students think a catalyst gives more
product, or that an inert gas always

99
00:04:30,349 --> 00:04:31,749
shifts the equilibrium.

100
00:04:32,149 --> 00:04:34,249
A catalyst changes only the speed.

101
00:04:34,249 --> 00:04:37,095
An inert gas at constant
volume changes nothing.

102
00:04:37,095 --> 00:04:41,004
At constant pressure, though, it
acts like lowering the pressure.

103
00:04:41,654 --> 00:04:43,043
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:43,043 --> 00:04:45,391
Boards ask the effect of each change.

105
00:04:45,391 --> 00:04:49,594
Entrance tests ask about the Haber
process, inert gases and catalysts.

106
00:04:50,194 --> 00:04:51,377
Exam level now.

107
00:04:51,377 --> 00:04:56,310
PC l five gas splits into PC l three
and chlorine, and takes in heat.

108
00:04:56,310 --> 00:04:59,718
Which change gives more
PC l three at equilibrium?

109
00:04:59,718 --> 00:05:00,951
A, a catalyst.

110
00:05:00,951 --> 00:05:02,435
B, higher pressure.

111
00:05:02,435 --> 00:05:04,069
C, higher temperature.

112
00:05:04,069 --> 00:05:05,553
D, adding chlorine.

113
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:06,786
Pause and solve.

114
00:05:12,186 --> 00:05:13,531
The answer is C.

115
00:05:13,531 --> 00:05:14,908
Higher temperature.

116
00:05:15,408 --> 00:05:17,647
The forward reaction takes in heat.

117
00:05:17,647 --> 00:05:20,227
Heating pushes it forward, and K grows.

118
00:05:20,727 --> 00:05:24,576
Higher pressure favours fewer
molecules, so it moves backward.

119
00:05:24,576 --> 00:05:27,093
Adding chlorine also pushes it backward.

120
00:05:27,593 --> 00:05:29,671
A catalyst only gets there faster.

121
00:05:29,671 --> 00:05:31,245
A is the common mistake.

122
00:05:31,845 --> 00:05:33,386
Here is your recap.

123
00:05:33,386 --> 00:05:34,970
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:38,170 --> 00:05:40,356
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:40,756 --> 00:05:41,656
One.

126
00:05:41,258 --> 00:05:42,823
You add more reactant.

127
00:05:42,823 --> 00:05:45,196
Which way does the equilibrium move?

128
00:05:45,996 --> 00:05:46,896
Two.

129
00:05:46,391 --> 00:05:48,738
Why does high pressure
help the Haber process?

130
00:05:49,538 --> 00:05:50,438
Three.

131
00:05:50,096 --> 00:05:51,742
Does a catalyst change K?

132
00:05:52,642 --> 00:05:56,188
Next time: why is lemon juice
sour, and soap slippery?

133
00:05:56,188 --> 00:05:58,770
We find out in Acids, Bases and Salts.
